<DocLayout title="Modes" eyebrow="Pick the confidence level" description="Axiom has two canonical modes. Select one deliberately; removed and legacy names are not accepted." next={{ label: "Use the API", href: "/api/" }}>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>basic</code></td><td>The default single-level matcher for local durable service or direct in-memory use.</td><td>Supported; qualification active</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>multilevel</code></td><td>The recursive hierarchy matcher for local durable service or direct in-memory use.</td><td>Available; resource qualification currently fails at startup</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Both modes use the same SQLite-backed <code>Durable</code> and thread-safe local <code>Service</code> path. <code>basic</code> is the default; choose <code>multilevel</code> explicitly when you need the recursive hierarchy. Neither mode is deployment-qualified. The latest constrained run fails Basic recovery and Multilevel startup—see <a class="text-link" href="../production/">current alpha status</a>.</p>

  <h2><code>basic</code></h2>
  <p>The single-level path maintains one z-system and its matching state. It is the best mode for learning the API, writing integration tests, and establishing a baseline for experiments.</p>
  <pre><code>matcher = Matcher(n=100, mode="basic")</code></pre>

  <h2><code>multilevel</code></h2>
  <p>The multilevel path recursively derives hierarchy levels and maintains level-specific state. Its public name is now canonical: use <code>mode="multilevel"</code>, not a compatibility alias.</p>
  <pre><code>matcher = Matcher(n=100, mode="multilevel")</code></pre>
  <p>For larger graphs, the hierarchy uses decreasing thresholds and phase schedules. The implementation validates shared graph identity, level ordering, deferred deletions, and the cross-level invariant before exposing refreshed state.</p>
  <div class="doc-callout"><p><strong>Do not over-read the research claim.</strong> Axiom exposes the multilevel engine for implementation and evaluation. The complete ABB+26 asymptotic guarantee is not claimed until the remaining paper-faithful coloring and end-to-end validation work is complete.</p></div>

  <h2>What is deliberately unsupported</h2>
  <p>Only the two documented constructor modes are accepted. Invalid mode names fail clearly so configuration mistakes are visible immediately; there is no forwarding constructor or wrapper API.</p>
</DocLayout>
